          "content": "A synthetic sheet-metal forming trial shows no sharp yield point, then undergoes a reverse loading cycle. An analyst applies the first-loading stress curve to predict springback after reversal. This fictional situation will be revisited in the integrated project. Start by distinguishing direct observations from physical descriptions and from parameters that a mathematical model proposes to infer. Write a list of controlled quantities, including applied load or displacement, specimen temperature, fixture constraints and the observation interval. Do not assume that a smooth stress\u2013strain curve establishes homogeneous deformation or excludes hidden failure mechanisms.\n\nConstruct 0.2 percent offset proof stress, separate plastic from elastic strain, evaluate a work-hardening fit on the admissible range, and compare an isotropic versus a kinematic-hardening illustration. The mathematics provides a disciplined way to compare outcomes but still depends on geometry, load history and the meaning of stress and strain. Across the five lessons, students should practice setting up a concrete force or energy balance, evaluating at least one numerical scale, and proposing a measurement that would discriminate among mechanisms. A complete argument identifies what a result cannot establish, even when a plotted trend agrees with an intuitive expectation.",
