          "content": "A fictional structural plate has several mapped surface indications and a load estimate with substantial uncertainty. A simplified screening calculation uses one flaw size and a constant geometry factor. This fictional situation will be revisited in the integrated project. Start by distinguishing direct observations from physical descriptions and from parameters that a mathematical model proposes to infer. Write a list of controlled quantities, including applied load or displacement, specimen temperature, fixture constraints and the observation interval. Do not assume that a smooth stress\u2013strain curve establishes homogeneous deformation or excludes hidden failure mechanisms.\n\nEvaluate K for several flaw-size and stress combinations; use G=K\u00b2/E-prime only with the correct elastic assumptions. Sketch a growth resistance curve and discuss unstable crack extension. The mathematics provides a disciplined way to compare outcomes but still depends on geometry, load history and the meaning of stress and strain. Across the five lessons, students should practice setting up a concrete force or energy balance, evaluating at least one numerical scale, and proposing a measurement that would discriminate among mechanisms. A complete argument identifies what a result cannot establish, even when a plotted trend agrees with an intuitive expectation.",
