          "content": "A polymer seal loses clamp load at a moderate temperature while a nearby metallic fixture accumulates sustained-load deformation. The two behaviors are mistakenly represented by the same stress\u2013strain plot. This fictional situation will be revisited in the integrated project. Start by distinguishing direct observations from physical descriptions and from parameters that a mathematical model proposes to infer. Write a list of controlled quantities, including applied load or displacement, specimen temperature, fixture constraints and the observation interval. Do not assume that a smooth stress\u2013strain curve establishes homogeneous deformation or excludes hidden failure mechanisms.\n\nCalculate an illustrative activated creep rate and a Maxwell relaxation curve, clearly specifying whether force or displacement is controlled. Compare time scales with the service interval. The mathematics provides a disciplined way to compare outcomes but still depends on geometry, load history and the meaning of stress and strain. Across the five lessons, students should practice setting up a concrete force or energy balance, evaluating at least one numerical scale, and proposing a measurement that would discriminate among mechanisms. A complete argument identifies what a result cannot establish, even when a plotted trend agrees with an intuitive expectation.",
