          "secNumber": "8.1",
          "heading": "Physical map and learning outcomes",
          "content": "A notional safety-critical component has candidate metal, polymer and ceramic options. Each has an attractive laboratory property and a critical missing datum, including fatigue uncertainty, time dependence or flaw statistics. This fictional situation will be revisited in the integrated project. Start by distinguishing direct observations from physical descriptions and from parameters that a mathematical model proposes to infer. Write a list of controlled quantities, including applied load or displacement, specimen temperature, fixture constraints and the observation interval. Do not assume that a smooth stress\u2013strain curve establishes homogeneous deformation or excludes hidden failure mechanisms.\n\nCreate a decision matrix using stiffness, strength, reliability, manufacturability and uncertainty. Demonstrate that missing evidence can dominate a small predicted performance advantage. The mathematics provides a disciplined way to compare outcomes but still depends on geometry, load history and the meaning of stress and strain. Across the five lessons, students should practice setting up a concrete force or energy balance, evaluating at least one numerical scale, and proposing a measurement that would discriminate among mechanisms. A complete argument identifies what a result cannot establish, even when a plotted trend agrees with an intuitive expectation.",
